Context: Discussion revealed original Clojure implementation in .hg/ directory

During analysis of the wave-client-for-emacs repository, we discovered that the original
server implementation was written in Clojure and exists in the Mercurial history within
the .hg/store/data/ directory. This finding highlighted the need for a more structured
research approach rather than continuing with ad-hoc exploration.

Need for structured research approach instead of ad-hoc exploration

The complexity of the Wave protocol and the distributed nature of the original codebase
(spanning Clojure server, Emacs Lisp client, and various protocol implementations)
requires systematic investigation rather than random deep-dives that could lead to
incomplete understanding or missed connections.

Goal: Enable `make -C experiments run` for coordinated experiment execution

The experiments infrastructure provides a standardized way to execute research tasks
in a repeatable manner. Each experiment has clear objectives, methodology, and expected
outputs, allowing for systematic knowledge building and easy reproduction of findings.

Strategy: Breadth-first exploration to avoid going "full DFS" on any single path

Rather than diving deep into any single aspect (like only the Clojure code or only the
Emacs client), the experiments are designed to build broad understanding across all
components first. This prevents getting lost in implementation details before
understanding the overall architecture and relationships.

Infrastructure: Master Makefile coordinates individual experiment execution

The master experiments/Makefile provides:
- Centralized experiment discovery and execution
- Consistent interface across all experiments
- Ability to run experiments individually or in sequence
- Standardized cleanup and maintenance operations
- Clear documentation of available research paths

This infrastructure supports the research methodology of systematic exploration while
maintaining flexibility for iterative refinement of individual experiments.
